WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.040
T.J. Miller is the breakout star of HBO's
tech satire Silicon Valley, stealing scenes

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.140
as the obnoxiously aggressive—and aggressively
obnoxious—Erlich Bachman:

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.099
"I know that you look at me and see a guy
who has it all figured out.

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:16.600
And for the most part you're right."

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.360
"Argh!"

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.260
"Ya, know half of the yogurt's gonna go unused
because one quarter of the jam can't come

00:00:22.269 --> 00:00:23.269
out!"

00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:26.779
He's hitting big at the movies, too, with
a major role as Weasel in the blockbuster

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:27.779
Deadpool.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:30.759
Here's a look back at where you might have
seen Miller before.

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:31.800
Carpoolers

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.280
Miller's first major role came via this short-lived
ABC single-camera comedy.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.960
Created by The Kids in the Hall's Bruce McCulloch,
it followed the lives of four guys connected

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.829
only by their daily carpool.

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:45.670
Miller played the son of one of carpoolers,
a gigantic but soft-spoken man-baby named

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:49.860
Marmaduke who still lives at home, doesn't
work, and spends most of his time entertaining

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.199
his own flights of whimsy.

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:52.199
"Hey Bob!"

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:54.090
"Father, I'm no longer Bob.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.700
Although, on some level I did feel closer
to you as Bob, there's just too many things

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.010
going on on the planet to be ignored."

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:02.500
Cloverfield

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.890
Very little was known about Cloverfield before
it hit theaters—including the cast.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.860
Miller plays a bro named Hud in this J.J.
Abrams-produced, found-footage style science-fiction

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.231
thriller about a group of college-age friends
having a party in New York City—until they're

00:01:15.231 --> 00:01:18.590
terrorized by a Godzilla-type monster creature
and its horrifying minions.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.110
"I don't know, something else!

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:21.110
Also terrible!"

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.720
While you don't see Miller's face through
most of Cloverfield, that's only because his

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.800
character is the guy holding the camera and
narrating the movie the whole time.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.940
In the end, though, things just don't work
out too well for old Hud...

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.540
(screams)

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.070
Chelsea Lately

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:42.490
Carpoolers was cancelled after 13 episodes
and the star of Cloverfield was the monster,

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:45.370
so Miller still had some career climbing to
do.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:49.360
Undaunted, Miller turned to stand up and sketch
comedy and made more than three dozen appearances

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.530
on Chelsea Lately, Chelsea Handler's talk
show on E!, showing up to do panel and also

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:54.530
appearing in sketches:

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:55.030
"T.J.

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.520
I don't see any stuffed animals."

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.780
"Well, Brad I don't know what to tell you,
ok?

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.440
I don't know what your prescription is, I
don't what the, ya know-"

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.760
"What, what are you doing?"

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.460
"It's a facial mist ever heard of it?"

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.300
He even guest-hosted the show a few times:

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.260
"I mean have you ever seen something like
that little golden rhino?"

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:13.860
Extract

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.840
This comedy was written and directed by Mike
Judge, who also made Beavis and Butthead and

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.090
Office Space, and is built around the owner
of a flavor extract company desperately trying

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:23.670
to cheat on his wife.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.090
Miller plays Rory, a dim worker at the extract
plant.

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.170
"What are we gonna do?

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.530
I got car payments, they're gonna take away
my Geo, they've done it before.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:31.530
We gotta act fast!"

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.340
It's a bit part, but it introduced Miller
to Judge, who would later cast him on Silicon

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.950
Valley.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.190
Yogi Bear

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:42.780
The 2010 big screen version of Yogi Bear was
meant to appeal to kids largely ignorant of

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.510
the more than 50-year-old cartoon character.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.070
The movie's notable for Miller's portrayal
of Ranger Jones, a role he secured with an

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:50.950
unhinged audition video.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:54.070
"Hey, hey Yogi what are you doing?

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.640
Ya that's what I thought, have you been stealing
picnic baskets?"

00:02:58.640 --> 00:02:59.640
(bear growl)

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:00.640
"You have?

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.030
Now you've been told not to do that, where's
Boo Boo?"

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.800
(bear growl)

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.340
"Well tell him to come back here!"

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.150
Miller sent Warner Bros. a video of himself
dressed up like a ranger and running lines

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.030
with a bear he rented for the day.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.250
Method acting at its finest.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:20.200
"Ranger Smith, I just wanted to say I'm sorry,
I wanna be in charge so badly, no no, hold

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.200
on now..."

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:22.390
The Goodwin Games

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:26.170
During a short lived Fox sitcom called The
Goodwin Games, Miller appeared as loveable

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.800
slacker Jimmy Goodwin.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.570
Through a series of videos left by their late
father, three estranged adult siblings return

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:35.220
to their small town thinking they're going
to inherit their father's millions…but it's

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.959
really just a plot to bring them closer together.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:41.920
Created by Carter Bays and Craig Thomas, who
also created How I Met Your Mother, The Goodwin

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.940
Games had its initial order cut to just six
episodes, which aired in the summer of 2013.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.030
Transformers: Age Of Extinction

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.230
How do you know you've really made it in Hollywood?

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.980
When you're cast as the comic relief in a
Michael Bay action movie.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:54.480
"Ok!

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:54.980
Ok!

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:55.480
Alright!

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:55.980
Ok!

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:56.480
Alright!

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:56.980
I was wrong!"

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:01.980
In 2014, Miller appeared in Transformers:
Age of Extinction as Lucas Flannery, mechanic

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.350
and best friend of Mark Wahlberg.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:04.350
"Get in here!

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:05.350
Lock that door!"

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:06.630
"It doesn't have a lock."

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:11.020
While it's kind of a tall order to compete
with dinosaur robots and a metal samurai,

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.540
Miller still managed to offer some standout
moments.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:15.700
"What kind of cars are those?!

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:16.840
They're so scary!"

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.600
Of course, that made his character's "death
by robo-grenade" all the more tragic.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.680
(epic screaming)

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:26.410
The Crunchies

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.290
Also called the "Oscars of Silicon Valley,"
the tech industry hands out awards to itself

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.080
at each year's "Crunchies."

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.310
Because of his role on Silicon Valley-

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:34.630
"My wife picked these out."

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.560
"Then you married poorly."

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.630
-Miller was asked to host in 2015, and organizers
thought he'd bring some fun to the proceedings:

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:42.810
"Bring him!

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.520
Does he not have a way to come?

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.570
Tell him to get an Uber!"

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:55.320
He did...but he also spent a lot of time making
fun of Gabi Holzwarth, a violinist who plays

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.120
tech industry parties…who is also the girlfriend
Uber founder Travis Kalanick.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.940
Holzwarth and Miller got into a shouting match,
and Miller made some ill-advised comments

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.260
before moving on to mock the many venture
capitalists in the audience.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.430
"Come on!

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.180
Get into it!

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.000
Act like you mean it!"

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.090
Thanks for watching!

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:16.570
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.880
And leave us a comment to let us know which
T.J. Miller appearance is your favorite.

